Account for rework yield and resource load without creating items (@engineering-production-rework: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "Derive-inventory-time-from-individual-residence-intervals.lingua#r_007AFDCF49CEB99954FFE85139") { r_009A472FF42DC85607FFCD97B2
A failed first inspection can lead to rework or scrap. Rework may recover useful output but consumes time, capacity and other resources. Begin with mutually exclusive paths rather than multiplying unrelated percentages. In a separate aggregate model let f be the fraction accepted on the first AB pass, r the fraction of first-pass rejects sent through one more AB pass, and g the fraction of those attempts finally accepted. There is at most one attempt. These are conditional fractions with specified denominators; they are not necessarily independent probabilities.

The four outcome fractions are f for first acceptance, (1−f)(1−r) for direct scrap, (1−f)rg for recovered acceptance, and (1−f)r(1−g) for scrap after rework. They sum to one by distributing the two conditional splits. Final accepted fraction is y=f+(1−f)rg and scrap fraction is 1−y. Each released item consumes one initial visit to each machine; the fraction (1−f)r adds one visit to each. Thus mean visits per released item at each resource are v=1+(1−f)r. Keep y and v distinct.

For an exact eight-item completed batch with first acceptance six, two rework attempts and one recovered item, f=3/4,r=1,g=1/2. There are seven accepted items and one final scrap. Each machine makes ten visits. Counting the two attempted reworks as two new releases would break the item balance. Counting the recovered acceptance as an additional item without subtracting its earlier reject status would count the same item twice.

If f,r,g are probabilities for a single hypothetical item, enumerate these four outcomes and weight their visit counts 1,1,2,2. Linearity of expectation gives the same v without requiring independence between different items. It does not imply that a finite batch contains its expected number of good pieces or that all rework is completed before a deadline. Integer batch fractions require compatible counts. The supplied checker verifies the algebra against independently weighted outcomes for 125 fraction triples; no factory defect distribution is estimated.

Under an additional ideal aggregate regime with completed flows, sufficient timing/buffering and unchanged conditional fractions, a resource with C visits per unit time supports release rate at most C/v and useful rate at most Cy/v. For the eight-item proportions y/v=7/10. If every rejected first-pass item were instead scrapped, useful output per visit would be f=3/4, which is greater. Rework increases accepted output per raw item from 3/4 to 7/8 yet decreases accepted output per constrained machine visit from 3/4 to 7/10. A raw-material-limited decision and a machine-time-limited decision can therefore differ.

This comparison excludes differences in material cost, repair skill, energy, final quality, environmental effects and future process improvement. It does not recommend discarding repairable products. A complete design must state which constraints and objectives apply. In our finite scheduler, routes and final labels are prescribed by scenario; it does not choose r based on noisy inspection. Introducing a decision after inspection requires observation states and policies rather than knowledge of the unobserved future.

**Read and work:** compare the selected inspection/action and performance passages in [Manufacturing Systems Analysis MIT 2 852](../sources/Manufacturing-Systems-Analysis-MIT-2-852.lingua#r_00185E34168A41C88CE14E1828) with the distinct denominators here. Use [Turn an intended use into requirements and observable evidence](Turn-an-intended-use-into-requirements-and-observable-evidence.lingua#r_0C3988C75ECA342B8BFC943458) to state an accepted-output criterion before computing yield. Derive the general condition y/v>f, treating r=0 and f=1 separately; for positive rework fraction it reduces to g>f. Extend the model to rework using a different resource, and identify the new capacity inequalities. Then challenge it with waiting-dependent defects or imperfect inspection, explaining which assumptions and evidence must change.

